<!--
    kuna option bejoin (off by default, GH-904; on here): SPARC returns a long long in %o0:%o1 with the
    HIGH word in %o0.  restore copies every in-register back to its
    out-register, so %o1 also comes back from functions that return one int,
    holding whatever the function left in %i1 (kuna-bejoin-sparc.xml).  These
    functions put their low word in %o1 on purpose, and it is part of the
    value:

      * through restore's own destination register: restore %g0,1,%o1 is
        tmp = 0 + 1; <window copies>; %o1 = tmp.  The last copy is the
        compiler's choice of register, not the window's.
      * in %i1 for restore to hand back, read by nothing but the returned
        registers: mov 10,%i1 / mov 20,%i1 before ret; restore %g0,%g0,%o0,
        and mix2's product (umul %i0,3,%i1; rd %y,%i0; ret; restore).

    Joined first register low, as before the fix:

        k_one:     return 0x100000000;         (status64 and bool64 at -O2 too)
        sel_const: return (uint8)v1 << 0x20;   (10 or 20 in the high word)
        mix2:      return a0;                  (for (u64)x << 32)
        mix3:      return 5;                   (for 0x500000000)

    neg_one, all ones, prints the same either way; an earlier version of the
    fix returned it as a 32-bit -1, which #1 and #1b guard against.

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype), option bejoin on.
    Little-endian targets are byte-identical; the default (off) pass and the
    trade-off the option makes are in kuna-bejoin.xml.

    Source (clang 14 -fintegrated-as, sparc-linux-gnu; bytes from
    bejoin_window64_sparc32_O2.o at 0x40000 and bejoin_window64_sparc32_O0.o
    at 0x50000, whose names end in _O0; the unrelocated calls to ext and
    ext_i print as sub_*):
        i64 neg_one(void) { ext(); return -1; }
        i64 k_one(void) { ext(); return 1; }
        i64 status64(int x) { ext(); if (x) return 1; return 0; }
        u64 bool64(int a, int b) { ext_i(a); return a == b; }
        i64 sel_const(int c) { ext(); return c ? 10 : 20; }
        u64 mix2(unsigned x, int c) { ext(); if (c) return (u64)x << 32; return (u64)x * 3; }
        u64 mix3(unsigned x, int c) { ext(); if (c) return 0x500000000ULL; return (u64)x * 3; }

    Assert #1: neg_one returns the 64-bit -1 at both levels, never 0xffffffff.
    Assert #2: k_one, status64 and bool64 return 64 bits at -O2.
    Assert #3: k_one (both levels), status64 and bool64 (-O2) return 1, not 0.
    Assert #4: sel_const keeps its argument and returns 10 or 20, never
    shifted into the high word, at both levels.
    Assert #5: mix2 returns x << 32 whole, and nothing returns a0 or 5.
    Assert #6: mix3 returns 0x500000000 whole.
    Assert #7: mix2 and mix3 join the product with its high word first.
-->
